 ## Background
-Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) were introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1, S3]. The method represents a continuous volumetric scene representation using a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) that maps 3D spatial coordinates and viewing directions to volume density and emitted color [S1, S3]. This approach solved the problem of high-quality novel view synthesis from a sparse set of 2D images by leveraging differentiable volume rendering to train the implicit representation [S1, S2]. Prior to NeRF, explicit representations like voxel grids or meshes required significant memory or manual geometry processing, while previous implicit methods lacked the capacity to model complex view-dependent effects [S1][S4, unofficial]. NeRF's use of positional encoding and hierarchical sampling allowed it to capture high-frequency details and realistic lighting, setting a new standard for neural rendering [S1, S3].
